74 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425674 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6f9f671-11c9-5940-b954-9ff8e3b7a115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c9cd435-4b0a-516c-bede-18c713c259dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425675 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-5{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-15t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3df34aa2-66f8-53fb-b84c-e23ff9464f43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425676 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46096919-32d4-552d-ad62-e0e1723dd83b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425677 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-4x=-4(-2)=8\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{3}=(2)^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d750ad2f9ea2a85446b94f7b19009ef74eeea25b4ccd1bf8ff4f408a5df381e2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3}+3, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b957da1-0df2-5626-98d6-1a7c5a3632dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425678 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d30d760181c95dd0764b9959ca91febb34e94618bb147d8022dae5745c38a1a.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}((x+2)^{3}+3)=(0)^{3}+3=3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+1)=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcdf5654322a6deff9d7598590b4e0e1ace6150020b4944ad844976fb0f70230.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8bfe2ba-a961-5858-9456-3382dda97cd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425679 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-7{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-\\frac{7}{3}t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4b9a1ad-55e6-52d3-8f59-ef1cc199427a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425680 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6x$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=3{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9666624-6b3b-5d5b-bd5e-3f9ae5e53130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425681 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [3{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-4,4).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension of $G(x)=x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is  \n{{field-2}}\nand  \n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\nx∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa5cac21-63bb-59e7-aa34-0b7a399b8755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425682 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-4,4),$\n\n- continuous on $[-4,4),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-4)=F(4),$ if we extend the value at $x=4$ using the formula $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is\n$\\text{continuous}$ and\n$\\text{piecewise smooth}$\non $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{20}{3})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}(-\\frac{n\\pi }{4}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
